WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:05.030
This episode of Real Engineering is brought
to you by Skillshare, home to over 16,000

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:07.410
classes that could teach you a new life skill.

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:12.530
By the time we reach our 80s, our hearts have
beat over 3.3 billion times and and have pumped

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:19.150
250 million litres of blood around the body,
enough blood to fill 100 olympic sized pools.

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:24.130
Just 3 weeks after conception the first muscle
cells of the heart begin to contract and don’t

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:26.060
stop until the moment we die.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:29.420
It is quite frankly a marvel that our hearts
can last so long.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:33.220
Pick up a stress ball and see how many times
you can squeeze it before arm starts to cramp

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:34.220
and tire.

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:38.300
Unlike skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle has
much higher numbers of mitochondria, which

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:42.730
provide the vital energy needed for contractions,
and a rich supply of oxygenated blood, it

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:47.310
never has to worry about the build of lactic
acid from working when deprived of oxygen.

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:50.540
But just because they do not fatigue does
not mean they are invincible.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:54.500
Heart disease is one of the leading causes
of death in the world, as we grow older some

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:58.180
parts of the heart can deteriorate and cause
some problems, but we have developed some

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:01.410
incredible technology that has extended our
lives.

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:05.840
One of the most common implants in the world
being pacemakers, small implantable devices

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:09.350
that help keep our hearts beating when our
natural systems need some help.

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:13.299
Before seeing how they are implanted and how
they work, let’s first see how our own bodies

00:01:13.299 --> 00:01:16.670
have been engineered by nature to keep our
blood flowing.

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:20.220
The heart has two sides separated by an inner
wall called the septum.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:23.829
The right side of the heart, which on this
diagram is on the left, pumps blood to the

00:01:23.829 --> 00:01:27.850
lungs where it is oxygenated and then travels
back to the heart where it is pumped by the

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:29.810
left side to the rest of the body.

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:33.619
The walls of the left side are much thicker
and stronger because of it has to pump blood

00:01:33.619 --> 00:01:37.719
around the entire body, whereas the right
side just has to pump it to the lungs and

00:01:37.719 --> 00:01:38.719
back.

00:01:38.719 --> 00:01:42.069
The heart consists of 4 chambers the left
and right ventricles and the left and right

00:01:42.069 --> 00:01:45.909
atria, the atria and ventricles are separated
by valves.

00:01:45.909 --> 00:01:50.409
At the start of the heart beat all 4 chambers
are relaxed and the valves are open.

00:01:50.409 --> 00:01:54.749
Blood flows into the heart from large veins
and the heart reaches max capacity.

00:01:54.749 --> 00:01:59.200
Just here there are a bundle of cells called
the Sinoatrial node that are capable of producing

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:03.670
a electric impulse that will travel through
the heart and cause it to contract in a specific

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:04.700
order.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:10.130
First the impulse travel to the AV node located
here, where it triggers both atria to contract,

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:13.530
squeezing blood out of the atria into the
left and right ventricles.

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:17.219
The electric impulse has now travelled down
through the heart to fibres located in the

00:02:17.219 --> 00:02:22.549
ventricle walls that now cause them to contract,
as the pressure rises it forces these valves

00:02:22.549 --> 00:02:25.400
to shut and prevents blood from flowing backwards.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:29.360
You can see each of these stages on the classic
electrocardiogram.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:34.250
The first little bump is the atria contracting,
followed by the spike of the stronger ventricles

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:38.579
contracting and finally there is another little
bump as ventricles recover.

00:02:38.579 --> 00:02:42.569
For most, this sequence of events goes unnoticed
thousands of times a day.

00:02:42.569 --> 00:02:46.469
But some may need a little help controlling
their heart beat. sometimes the Sinoatrial

00:02:46.469 --> 00:02:51.079
node’s ability to set the correct pace breaks
down, leading to slower heartbeats or long

00:02:51.079 --> 00:02:52.790
pauses between heartbeats.

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:57.170
There are a range of reasons why you may need
help and this is why the incredible pacemaker

00:02:57.170 --> 00:02:58.480
was invented.

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:03.709
And as with most medical inventions it started
as an incredibly dangerous and scary device.

00:03:03.709 --> 00:03:07.440
The first being invented by Albert S Hyman
in the 1930s.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:11.420
It consisted of a hand cranked spring motor,
which would store the hand crank rotation

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:13.720
as potential energy in a spring.

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:17.659
This spring motor then drove a generator,
with these large U-Shaped magnets providing

00:03:17.659 --> 00:03:21.140
the magnetic flux needed to generate a direct
current voltage.

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:25.689
The current was then pulsed by a rotating
interrupter disk with four conducting pads

00:03:25.689 --> 00:03:30.459
which intermittently made contact with a brush
which supplied this huge needle electrode.

00:03:30.459 --> 00:03:34.819
Amazingly this little machine was portable,
but it proved ineffective due to the low voltage

00:03:34.819 --> 00:03:35.980
output.

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:40.140
His work was ultimately abandoned, but other
researchers recognised its potential after

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:41.609
the second world war.

00:03:41.609 --> 00:03:46.319
A method of inducing hypothermia by cooling
the heart until it stopped beating to allow

00:03:46.319 --> 00:03:50.780
it to be worked on during surgery was being
investigated, but upon rewarming it was found

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:55.030
that the heart needed help with controlling
heart rate as metabolic function recovered.

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:59.709
And John A. Hopps developed this device for
the job, delivering impulses at the desired

00:03:59.709 --> 00:04:05.049
rate through paddles that were placed inside
the chest cavity during surgery near the Sinoatrial

00:04:05.049 --> 00:04:06.049
Node.

00:04:06.049 --> 00:04:10.390
The potential of using such a device on patients
suffering from heart defects at normal temperatures

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:15.420
was soon realised, but repeated applications
of high voltages cause muscle pain and twitches,

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:16.820
along with burns.

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:18.600
Making it unsuitable for extended use.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:21.319
What we needed was an implantable device.

00:04:21.319 --> 00:04:25.310
Hopps developed a catheter electrode, which
removed the need for open chest surgeries

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:29.180
by passing the electrodes through the subclavian
vein and into the heart.

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:35.240
On halloween night 1957 a power outage struck
a minneapolis hospital, leaving several young

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:40.039
patients without pacing from the mains powered
pacemakers, killing one of the children and

00:04:40.039 --> 00:04:46.120
pushing famed heart surgeon Dr. C Walton Lillehei
to request the hospitals technician Earl Bakken

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:48.350
to develop a battery powered pacemaker.

00:04:48.350 --> 00:04:53.530
He returned with this device, a wearable pacemaker
powered by mercury batteries that would launch

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:57.120
Earl Bakken’s company Medtronic into the
fortune 500.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:01.879
This was a revolutionary device, but the need
to pass the wires through the skin was a constant

00:05:01.879 --> 00:05:03.080
infection risk.

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:07.940
What was needed was a fully implantable device
and with continual improvements to transistors

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:12.560
allowing for the miniaturization of circuitry,
and improvements to batteries this was soon

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:13.560
realised.

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:18.629
This was the first ever fully implantable
pacemaker, created and implanted in Sweden

00:05:18.629 --> 00:05:21.090
to save Arne Larsson’s life.

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:25.129
With a rechargeable nickel-cadmium battery,
which was charged through this induction coil

00:05:25.129 --> 00:05:26.910
overnight about once a month.

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:32.060
It utilized some of the first silicon transistors
imported into Sweden, allowing it to use less

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:35.110
energy over older Germanium transistors.

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:38.590
All this was encapsulated in a biocompatible
epoxy resin.

00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:43.860
Arne Larsson survived to 86, with his pacemaker
being replaced a total of 25 times over the

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:47.690
course of his life, as the technology improved
incrementally. [5]

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:52.050
Current generation pacemakers are now smaller
and more reliable than ever.

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:56.000
Medtronic have even developed the world’s
smallest pacemaker which is implanted directly

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:00.370
into the right ventricle without any cables,
and eliminating the need for the pacemaker

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:03.720
to be implanted under the skin, which can
lead to discomfort.

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:08.210
Over 700,000 pacemakers are implanted worldwide
every year.

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:12.409
[2] These devices help people live healthier,
happier lives and have advanced so far that

00:06:12.409 --> 00:06:15.550
many can practically forget that they suffer
from heart disease.

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:19.220
